gaussIdeal -- correlation ideal of a Bayesian network of joint Gaussian variables
*********************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        gaussIdeal(R,G)
  * Inputs:
      * R, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, created with *note gaussRing:
        gaussRing,
      * G, an instance of the type Graph (missing documentation) or a *note
        list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,
  * Outputs:
      * the ideal in R of the relations in the correlations of the random
        variables implied by the independence statements of the graph G or the
        list of independence statements G

Description
===========

These ideals were first written down by Seth Sullivant, in "Algebraic geometry
of Gaussian Bayesian networks". The routines in this package involving Gaussian
variables are all based on that paper.
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = gaussRing 5;                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : G = makeGraph {{2},{3},{4,5},{5},{}}                                    |
|                                                                             |
|o2 = Graph{1 => set {2}   }                                                  |
|           2 => set {3}                                                      |
|           3 => set {4, 5}                                                   |
|           4 => set {5}                                                      |
|           5 => set {}                                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o2 : Graph                                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : (globalMarkovStmts G)/print;                                            |
|{{1, 2}, {4, 5}, {3}}                                                        |
|{{1}, {3, 4, 5}, {2}}                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : J = gaussIdeal(R,G)                                                     |
|                                                                             |
|o4 = ideal (- s   s    + s   s   , - s   s    + s   s   , - s   s    +       |
|               1,5 2,4    1,4 2,5     1,5 3,4    1,4 3,5     2,5 3,4         |
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|     s   s   , s   s    - s   s   , s   s    - s   s   , s   s    - s   s   ,|
|      2,4 3,5   1,4 2,3    1,3 2,4   1,4 3,3    1,3 3,4   2,4 3,3    2,3 3,4 |
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|     s   s    - s   s   , s   s    - s   s   , s   s    - s   s   , -        |
|      1,5 2,3    1,3 2,5   1,5 3,3    1,3 3,5   2,5 3,3    2,3 3,5           |
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|     s   s    + s   s   , - s   s    + s   s   , - s   s    + s   s   ,      |
|      1,4 2,3    1,3 2,4     1,5 2,3    1,3 2,5     1,5 2,4    1,4 2,5       |
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|     s   s    - s   s   , s   s    - s   s   , s   s    - s   s   )          |
|      1,3 2,2    1,2 2,3   1,4 2,2    1,2 2,4   1,5 2,2    1,2 2,5           |
|                                                                             |
|o4 : Ideal of R                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+


A list of independence statements (as for example returned by
globalMarkovStmts) can be provided instead of a graph.


The ideal corresponding to a conditional independence statement {A,B,C} (where
A,B,C, are disjoint lists of integers in the range 1..n (n is the number of
random variables) is the #C+1 x #C+1 minors of the submatrix of the generic
symmetric matrix M = (s_(i,j)), whose rows are in A union C, and whose columns
are in B union C.  In general, this does not need to be a prime ideal.
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : I = gaussIdeal(R,{{{1,2},{4,5},{3}}, {{1},{2},{3,4,5}}})                |
|                                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|o5 = ideal (- s   s    + s   s   , - s   s    + s   s   , - s   s    +       |
|               1,5 2,4    1,4 2,5     1,5 3,4    1,4 3,5     2,5 3,4         |
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
